<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iftikhar Hussain Khan Mamdot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iftikhar Hussain Khan Mamdot was a Pakistani politician from the family of the nawabs of Mamdot, among the largest landowners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. A leader of the Punjab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>, he became the first chief minister of West Punjab after independence in 1947, serving until 1949, and later served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iftikhar-hussain-khan-mamdot.jpg" alt="Iftikhar Hussain Khan Mamdot" width="157" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammad_Ali_Jinnah_and_Nawab_Iftikhar_Hussain_Khan_Mamdot_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mamdot was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 31 December 1906, the son of Shahnawaz Khan Mamdot, into the Kheshgi family. He was educated at Government College, Lahore, and then joined the police service of Hyderabad State in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. On his father's death in 1942 he succeeded him as Nawab of Mamdot and as President of the Punjab Muslim League, a post he held until 1944, and he worked to draw the great landowners of the Punjab away from the Unionist Party and towards the Pakistan Movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected to the Punjab Legislative Assembly in 1946, he became leader of the opposition, and that year he was the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> leader in the Punjab to back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a>'s call for a voluntary exchange of populations. At Partition he left his estates in eastern Punjab, which became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and on 15 August 1947 he was appointed chief minister. To rebuild his following he set up, without official sanction, an Allotment Revising Committee among the refugees, and his opposition to the reformist resettlement proposals of his minister Mian Iftikharuddin led to the minister's resignation.</p><p>His ministry was torn by feuds with Ghazanfar Ali Khan, Mumtaz Daultana and the governor, Sir Francis Mudie, whom he called a foreigner and pro-Indian, while Mudie accused him of staying in power to acquire property. It was alleged that he used public funds to obtain about 2,000 acres in Montgomery District at nominal rates and moved money from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> fund to his brother's account. He resigned in 1949, an official enquiry into maladministration followed, and the governor took direct control of the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1950 he left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> to found the Jinnah Muslim League, which fought the 1951 elections against the League led by Daultana. He rejoined the Muslim League in 1953, and Malik Ghulam Muhammad appointed him Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> in 1954. He resigned in 1955 when Ghulam Muhammad left the political scene and spent his remaining years in the political wilderness. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on 16 October 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iftikhar Hussain Khan Mamdot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The family stayed prominent: <b>Iftikhar Hussain Khan Mamdot</b> became the first Chief Minister of West Punjab in Pakistan in 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/mamdot-state">Mamdot State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.55,74.34&amp;year=1946" aria-label="Lahore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6468 1015 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Iftikhar Hussain Khan Mamdot"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6918 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6918 1285h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.55,74.34&amp;year=1946">What was here in 1946? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
